<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=loom">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Tools and materials</p><h1 class="enc-title">Loom</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 4400 BCE</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A loom is a device for weaving cloth and tapestry that holds the warp threads under tension while the weft threads are passed over and under them. The earliest evidence of a horizontal loom is a picture on a pottery dish from ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> dated to 4400 BCE, showing a frame loom with treadles that left the weaver's hands free to pass and beat the weft. The warp-weighted loom, whose warp is kept taut by hanging weights, may go back to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>. The drawloom for patterned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> is first confirmed by fabrics from the State of Chu of about 400 BCE; it sped up Chinese silk weaving and was introduced to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/loom.jpg" alt="Loom" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: G-Lignum · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Loom_2,_Lesbos_(Skala_Sykamias).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 4400 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Egypt</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The warp-weighted loom may have originated earlier, in the Neolithic period.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=loom">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
